---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../../components/sections/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../../components/sections/Footer.astro'
import BlogArt from '../../components/blog/BlogArt.astro'
import Byline from '../../components/blog/Byline.astro'
import SeriesNav from '../../components/blog/SeriesNav.astro'
import { postBySlug, formatPostDate, postSocialImage } from '../../data/blog'

const post = postBySlug('what-desire-builds')!
// Personal scenes come from Chris's account on 22 October 2026. Research and
// branch-specific implementation evidence are recorded in exploration 0483.
---

<Base
  title={`${post.title} — xNet`}
  description={post.description}
  image={postSocialImage(post).src}
  imageAlt={postSocialImage(post).alt}
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border bg-surface/20">
      <BlogArt slug={post.slug} eager />
      <div
        class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-b from-black/10 via-black/65 to-black/95"
        aria-hidden="true"
      ></div>
      <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14 pt-44 lg:pt-52">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-xs text-amber-100">
          {post.draft ? <span>Draft for review</span> : <time datetime={post.pubDate}>{formatPostDate(post.pubDate)}</time>}
          <span aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>{post.readingMinutes} min read</span>
          {post.tags.map((tag) => (
            <span class="rounded-full border border-amber-200/30 bg-black/20 px-2 py-0.5 font-mono lowercase">
              {tag}
            </span>
          ))}
        </div>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white drop-shadow lg:text-5xl">
          {post.title}
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-200">{post.description}</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article
      class="prose prose-lg mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 dark:prose-invert prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-rose-700 dark:prose-a:text-rose-300"
    >
      <Byline post={post} />

      <p>
        Somewhere in adulthood, bathing myself changed. I couldn't tell you
        exactly when. The soap and the washcloth were still there, but what
        had been a chore began to feel like time I got to spend with my body.
      </p>
      <p>
        I started to massage instead of just scrub. To notice where I held
        tension, where I was soft, which parts felt rough or smooth. I'd hang
        out with a place for a while, from my toes all the way up to my scalp,
        and feel what was there. Getting clean became one part of a much
        larger thing I was doing.
      </p>
      <p>
        I used to prefer showers. Now I prefer baths. The change sounds so
        small that I almost don't know how to explain what it has meant to
        me. A body I had mostly treated as something to keep clean and strong
        became someone I could have a relationship with. Was it getting
        enough warmth? Where did it want softness, or movement, or a little
        more time?
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything started to matter. I could be curious about the whole
        territory instead of trying to improve one property of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That feeling has travelled. I used to be self-conscious in locker
        rooms. These days, a naked sauna in Berlin is one of my favourite
        kinds of places: the warmth, the ease of being around people who
        seem comfortable in their bodies. It feels cosy to me. I like being
        somewhere a body can be an ordinary thing to have.
      </p>
      <p>
        I've written about xNet as <a href="/blog/a-great-pirate-age">a pirate
        internet</a> and about <a href="/blog/data-should-work-like-soil">data
        as soil</a>. Those images help me think about freedom and what we
        might grow together. The bath brings the question closer. What kind
        of relationship do I want with the life those tools are meant to serve?
      </p>

      <h2 id="energy-that-wants-to-move">Energy that wants to move</h2>
      <p>
        When I say Eros, I mean something I can feel. Often it's a hum in my
        belly, below the solar plexus. Sexual energy, sacral energy: those
        are words I reach for. There's a sense of aliveness, of something
        wanting to move. It might become the wish to make something, cook,
        exercise, touch, or share an idea with someone.
      </p>
      <p>
        While talking through this essay, I was standing at my desk,
        wiggling around a little. I could feel my voice and notice which
        words felt true coming out of it. My attention would move to my
        belly, to what felt grounded there, and then back into speaking.
        Thinking about Eros was itself a physical experience. The story was
        taking shape through my whole body.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sex belongs in this. Desire for another person, the pleasure of
        touch, the vulnerability of being wanted or wanting someone who
        may not want the same thing: these are part of what I mean. I don't
        want to wash the sexuality out of Eros to make it respectable enough
        for a software blog.
      </p>
      <p>
        But some of my most profound intimate and sexual experiences have
        also held sadness, hurt, laughter, and longing. There was room for
        more of being human. With myself or another person, I could let a
        feeling arrive without immediately trying to push it away. That
        capacity to stay with what was moving felt connected to the same
        creative energy.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the language I have for my experience. I don't expect
        everyone to feel a hum in their belly, or to find a sexual language
        useful for their creativity. What interests me is the possibility
        of listening closely enough to discover what actually moves us.
      </p>
      <p>
        Audre Lorde's <a href="https://www.haverford.edu/sites/default/files/Center/HCAH/Audre-Lorde-Poetry-is-Not-a-Luxury.pdf#page=7" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Uses
        of the Erotic</em></a> gives this inquiry a deeper root. Writing as
        a Black lesbian feminist, she connects deeply felt joy with power,
        work, and the lives we allow ourselves to want. Her argument makes
        me wonder what would change if the quality of feeling mattered in
        our work as much as getting through it.
      </p>
      <p>
        I know how to ask whether software works. I'm trying to get better
        at asking what it feels like to live with it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="more-of-us-in-the-room">More of us in the room</h2>
      <p>
        Enki's <a href="https://medium.com/@enkiislove/on-an-emerging-temple-culture-59130efbb14e" rel="noopener noreferrer">essay
        on an emerging temple culture</a> helped bring these thoughts
        together. It imagines community with room for erotic aliveness,
        emotional expression, and expanded consciousness. I hear an
        invitation to bring more of ourselves into the room, including the
        parts that don't fit neatly into the usual roles.
      </p>
      <p>
        I want a life where work, play, friendship, and partnership can
        mingle. Lovers can make good work together. A colleague can become
        a close friend, and a serious conversation can make room for
        silliness. In family life, playfulness and being taken seriously
        can sit beside each other. We have more ways to meet than the
        categories on a form suggest.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's an innocence in that possibility that I find very dear.
        You get to be with someone in their creativity and watch it expand.
        Sometimes you're precise and focused; later you're laughing at
        something ridiculous. Both moments belong to the relationship.
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, this can have a spiritual quality: the sense that being
        here together deserves care. The person beside me might describe
        the same moment quite differently. I want room for that, too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bringing my whole self doesn't mean offering every part of myself
        to every person. A colleague may want a warm working relationship
        and nothing more. Someone can enjoy a shared sauna without inviting
        touch. The possibility of a richer relationship depends on being
        able to find out what the other person actually wants, without
        making their answer a test of how open they are.
      </p>
      <p>
        That matters especially where someone controls another person's
        pay or place in a community. I want belonging to survive a no.
        Nobody should have to offer intimacy to keep a job, or perform
        delight to keep a friendship. A person can also name harm and ask
        for help; spiritual language doesn't excuse what someone did.
      </p>

      <h2 id="an-answer-that-changes-things">An answer that changes things</h2>
      <p>
        Consent gives desire a way to meet another desire. I can ask you
        to dance because your answer gets to change what happens. You might
        want to talk instead, or sit alone. The next thing we do can grow
        from that answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Betty Martin's <a href="https://www.wheelofconsent.org/wheel" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wheel
        of Consent</a> asks us to distinguish who is doing something from
        whose enjoyment it serves. I find that a useful question to bring
        into making software. When I'm excited to build a feature,
        whose wish will it answer? What happens when the person using it
        wants less than I want to give them?
      </p>
      <p>
        A maker may want a growing audience while someone at the screen
        wants one good conversation. If the business needs that person to
        keep searching, satisfaction becomes inconvenient. That is the
        treadmill I want to watch for in my own work: making something
        that depends on people remaining unsatisfied.
      </p>
      <p>
        Una Lee and Dann Toliver's <a href="https://www.consentfultech.io/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/Building-Consentful-Tech.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Building
        Consentful Tech</em></a> carries consent into the making and use of
        technology. It belongs throughout the relationship. An invitation
        to share a document should say who can read it. Changing your mind
        should have a consequence. Sharing one thing should leave you able
        to keep another thing to yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Christopher Alexander and his collaborators describe an <a href="https://patternlanguage.cc/Patterns/Intimacy-Gradient-%28127%29" rel="noopener noreferrer">intimacy
        gradient</a> in buildings, moving from public spaces towards more
        private rooms. I can imagine a digital place with that quality.
        We could share the plans for a gathering, then choose smaller
        conversations with their own invitations. Staying in the common
        room would remain a complete way to belong.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-we-make-together">What we make together</h2>
      <p>
        This is becoming much more concrete in the direction I'm exploring
        for xNet. Conversation is moving to the centre. People can talk to
        each other, invite an AI agent to help, and gradually build the
        tools they want around what they're doing together.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is already a desktop prototype where a person can ask an
        agent for a small tool, try it beside the chat with chosen data,
        and ask for changes. They can keep versions and share source that
        someone else can review and adapt. Shared projects, where several
        people contribute requests around the same evolving tool, are
        still being developed. That's the direction I want to follow.
      </p>
      <p>
        Imagine a few friends planning a gathering. There's dancing,
        cooking, somewhere quiet to rest. Someone asks the agent for a
        little board to help organise the work. They try it. One person
        wants to coordinate rides; another sees the list of empty slots
        and feels as though coming to the party has become an obligation.
        They talk about it, change the invitation, and try again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The observation that changes the whole thing might come from
        someone who never writes a line of code. They know what it feels
        like to arrive tired, to want company while having little to give,
        or to be excited about dancing and nervous about being touched.
        Their experience has something to teach the design. The <a href="https://designjustice.org/read-the-principles" rel="noopener noreferrer">Design
        Justice Network</a> makes this a principle: people affected by a
        design should have a central voice in shaping it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is one way I can see software emerging from Eros. A wish
        finds words. Someone answers, an idea takes shape, and making it
        reveals things we couldn't have explained at the start. We get to
        encounter something together and feel our way towards what we
        want it to become.
      </p>
      <p>
        The electrons are part of that passage. Spoken words become
        symbols and instructions, carried out in physical machines. The
        hands, voices, and bodies haven't ceased to matter because the
        result is digital. They helped decide what the machine would do.
      </p>
      <p>
        An agent can help make an idea tangible quickly. The people still
        need time to meet what it made. A fluent answer cannot tell us
        whether someone feels welcome, wants touch, or likes the plan.
        The person who takes longer to find words may have the most
        important thing to say. Silence in a busy chat is a poor substitute
        for hearing from them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also like the proposal to show how a version was made: whose
        request started it, what feedback changed it, which ideas branched
        off. A group could remember its work together. That history needs
        choices about who can see it. A useful tool should be able to
        travel without carrying every vulnerable conversation that helped
        it come into being.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the introduction to <a href="https://lewishyde.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/GIFT-Introduction-1.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>The
        Gift</em></a>, Lewis Hyde describes how art can be received as a
        gift even when money changes hands. I think a tool can carry
        something of that quality. Someone takes what you made into a life
        you don't control and finds a use you hadn't imagined. You can be
        paid for the work and still leave the recipient free.
      </p>

      <h2 id="room-to-be-a-person">Room to be a person</h2>
      <p>
        I want xNet to have room for the range of people we are. Sexuality
        and intellect, playfulness and seriousness, anger and fear all
        belong to human life. Each person should have some say in which
        parts they bring to a particular encounter. A place can welcome
        that richness without asking everyone to become equally visible.
      </p>
      <p>
        That ambition reaches into small technical choices. Letting a
        tool read a note on my device is different from sending that note
        to an AI provider. Inviting someone into a project doesn't give
        them the use of my agent account. Sharing code needn't share my
        private records. The design work is to make those choices clear
        enough that people can actually make them.
      </p>
      <p>
        xNet's commitment to keeping a primary copy of your work on your
        own device helps make this possible, but it isn't a complete
        privacy promise. The chat prototype still has private-community
        security work to finish and verify. I can't yet offer it as a
        place to entrust intimate records. And even a finished access
        system can't erase a message someone already copied.
      </p>
      <p>
        These limits matter because I want closeness to be something
        people can choose. Our <a href="/commitments">charter</a>
        rejects relationship health scores and closeness rankings. I
        don't want a machine deciding how well we're loving each other.
        There's enough to do helping us make the things we ask for.
      </p>
      <p>
        Connection can happen through a screen. An exchange of letters,
        a late conversation across time zones, or a tool made together
        from separate homes can be part of an intimate life. Bodies have
        different needs and capacities. Putting a device down isn't the
        only way to honour them.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <a href="https://www.bostonreview.net/articles/pleasure-activism/" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Pleasure
        Activism</em></a>, adrienne maree brown asks what it means to have
        enough, with particular care for people whose pleasure has been
        denied by oppression. I want to bring that question into a
        network where we can make something useful and feel free to stop.
        If we stay, let it be for the company.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work still needs rest and wages. Some days it will be boring.
        Nobody owes a project their passion, and an exciting idea doesn't
        make exhaustion disappear. I want a way of making that has room
        for those truths, alongside the pleasure of discovering what we
        can do together.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <a href="/blog/timeout">Timeout</a>, I wrote about finding my
        way back towards my body. This feels like another part of that
        movement. I want an intimate relationship with my own life, with
        the curiosity and tenderness I want to bring to a partner. I'd
        like that relationship to have a say in what I build.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bath is still a bath. I get clean. I also get to spend time
        with myself, and I leave knowing a little more about what I need.
        It seems like a good place to begin making something for someone else.
      </p>

      <aside class="mt-14 border-t border-border pt-6 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400" aria-label="About this essay">
        <p>
          The gathering is imagined and the cover is an illustration.
          This essay draws on my own accounts and the linked readings,
          developed with Codex. Applying these ideas to xNet is my interpretation.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </article>
    <SeriesNav slug={post.slug} />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
